  "research_question": "RQ3",
  "model": "MobileNetV2",
  "findings": "Preprocessing (resize to 224x224 and backbone-specific normalization) is essential for transfer learning. Ablation on MobileNetV2 shows: (1) removing augmentation increased test accuracy from 93.3% to 96.2% on this fixed split, with sparse_green F1 rising from 0.79 to 0.89; (2) removing class weights alone yielded 95.2% accuracy; (3) the bare model (no aug, no weights) reached 97.5% test accuracy and highest macro-F1 (0.96). This suggests augmentation and class weights act as regularizers that can lower performance on a static test set while still improving robustness to real-world variation (rotation, lighting). For imbalanced greenery screening, class weights remain recommended when macro-F1 on minority classes (dense_green, moderate_green) is prioritized over overall accuracy.",
